Z||000009||Greg Piet||07-05-2001||05:28 AM||||Its perfectly legal if you run in the appropriate class...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>nationally/divisionally its the  SM2 class<BR>in my region it is put in SM since we allow 2 seat cars in SM if the 2 seat thing is the only reason why it doesnt meet SM rules.<P>PAX for SM is slightly better than CSP so even after the engine upgrade I got a better PAX adjustment regionally...<BR>It doesnt mean much for me anymore as I am going into F125 however.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||07-05-2001||09:03 AM||||I started with a used 3.1 from a Z 24. sent it to Jasper and got it worked over pretty good. internals are so small and lite, it really loves revs. The way I wind it up if it was a sb, id blow it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||ThatRickGuy||07-05-2001||11:10 AM||||I did the 3.1 rebuild last year. Basically, I got the car and the 2.8 was knockin real bad. I had a local shop do the rebuild, I just told them which parts to use. The rebuild cost about $1800 new pistons, rods, crank, cam, lifters, 3 angle valve job, blue printing, balancing and assembly. Between storage fees, other parts (starter, battery, ignition etc...) I did while it was out it came to arround $2150 total. Personally, if I had it to do over again, I could have just gotten a crate 3.4 from GM for $1600. <P>As for over all power. w/ the 3.2 (3.1 bored) ported manifolds, no cat, and high flow muffler, it's pretty spunky, rev's fast and sounds decent. Mine isn't running optimally, but it's almost there, just a little rich, still has good power, expecially above 3000rpms<P>anyways, email me if you have any questions, I think I still have part numbers and stuff||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||07-05-2001||02:04 PM||||a 'little' rich is fine. I learned from flying, recip engines valves will run cooler slightly rich. My AMG Mercedes was built to run rich to also keep from burning valves/pistons at sustained high speed. The little amount of hp loss is a good trade off.||||reg||1
